  (c)                                                                                                                                                           
  - 1.1 ("null deletes its key") — the null exemption sits at the top of  validateValue , so it also covers array items, not just "any field being patched":    
   {tags:["a",null]}  passes against  items:{type:"string"}  and  mergeState  stores a literal  null , though null is supposed to be "never a value" and        
  deletion has no meaning for an element.                                                                                                                       
  - 1.1 — nested deletion only works when the parent already exists.  applyPatch({}, {meta:{depth:null}}, schema)  returns  {meta:{depth:null}}  with           
  changed:true , because  mergeState  copies the patch subtree verbatim when  t.meta  is absent; the null is persisted instead of deleting the key.             
  - 1.3 (" $append  operator") —  isAppendOp  requires an array payload, so a malformed  {meta:{$append:"x"}}  against an object-typed key validates and        
  merges as a literal  $append  member, and  {findings:{$append:[…], note:"x"}}  validates while  mergeState  silently drops  note . The array-only             
  operator's invalid forms slide through instead of rejecting.                                                                                                  
  - 1.3 —  {findings:{$append:[]}}  on a state lacking the key creates  findings:[]  and reports  changed:true , contradicting the "an empty append changes     
  nothing" comment and flipping an otherwise idle step into a commit.                                                                                           
                                                                                                                                                                
